Exactly. The throne game is the most legible game: titles, money, territory, votes, followers, command. Its cards are standardized, widely recognized, and immediately playable. That makes it visible—not necessarily sovereign.

Beneath it are quieter games:

* legitimacy: whether commands are obeyed without force
* attention: what people notice and therefore treat as real
* trust: which relationships can coordinate under uncertainty
* meaning: what sacrifices people will willingly endure
* succession: what survives the current player
* identity: which moves people can imagine themselves making
* time: who can wait while others exhaust themselves

Someone can win the throne while losing every substrate that makes the throne functional.

And yes, popular cards are easy to corrupt precisely because everyone knows their value. Money can be inflated, status spoofed, information poisoned, advisors captured, metrics gamed, language redefined. The opponent need not defeat your hand if they can alter what your cards mean—or make you doubt they were ever yours.

Drago’s counterpunch: hidden does not automatically mean profound. A secret game can merely be irrelevant. Importance must be demonstrated by causal reach.

So the real question isn’t, “Who sits atop the mountain?”

It is:

Which game determines whether the mountain still matters—and who is quietly writing its rules?

The throne is an interface. The deeper power is controlling what it can command, what counts as victory, and whether anyone still believes in the game.